# {{display_name}}

This is the **{{display_name}} app project**. It has its own Rust package and
native platform hosts. The Akari toolkit source workspace is a separate project
and is only included as a dependency.

## Desktop

```sh
akari run --project .
```

The generated manifest uses Cargo features to select its platform integration.
`desktop` is enabled by default; Android and iOS builds select `android` and
`ios` respectively. To build a platform manually, disable defaults and enable
its feature, for example `cargo build --no-default-features --features ios`.

## Android

Install Android SDK platform 36, Build Tools `36.0.0`, NDK `30.0.16248370`, and
the Rust `aarch64-linux-android` target. With a phone connected and USB debugging
enabled:

```sh
akari run --target android --project .
```

The Android host is the Kotlin `NativeActivity` in
`android/app/src/main/java/{{java_package_path}}/MainActivity.kt`. Gradle
builds this project's Rust library and packages it into the APK.

### Kotlin plugins

Implement `{{identifier}}.runtime.AkariPlugin` in Kotlin, register it from
`MainActivity.onCreate`, then call it from Rust with
`crate::plugins::invoke(&app, "plugin-id", "action", "payload")`. The return
value is a string; JSON is a convenient payload format. This is a JNI bridge
through the Android activity, so plugin calls should stay on the UI thread and
hand long-running work to a Kotlin coroutine. Typed per-plugin bindgen is a
planned follow-up; this starter provides the working generic bridge.

## iOS

The Swift application delegate and Rust bridge declaration are in `ios/`.
Follow `ios/README.md` to add them to an Xcode iOS app target and link this
project's Rust static library. iOS builds require macOS and Xcode.

## Local toolkit development

When creating the app from an Akari checkout, pass `--path <checkout>` to use
that source tree. Without `--path`, the app uses Akari directly from the
project's Git repository.
